nobody has ever measured how much  plastic pollution is in the sea and on  the beaches here around the lammu  islands off kenya's northern coast  the campaigners on board this sailing  boat want to give it a go  it's called the flip-floppy and made  from recycled plastic waste named after  the plastic sandals that cover it we're  interested in understanding how we can  create a circular economy for plastic  waste here in in a local  environment like this so to do that we  have to understand how much plastic is  here we have to understand  how much people know about this issue  the flip-floppy crew members campaign  against the use of single-use plastics  and try to find ways for people to reuse  plastic waste  the flip floppy is the first sailing  boat of its kind in the world and this  survey is the first of its kind to ever  take place in this part of the indian  ocean the crew say they don't know  exactly how much plastic pollution is in  the ocean on the beaches or in the  mangroves but they say they're sure  that there's a lot  and they're right on every island  they've stopped plastic bottles litter  the mangrove swamps that line the shores  by roping off squares and collecting and  weighing the plastic in each  they can estimate how many tons are here  and then share ideas with the  communities of what to do with it all  my moon shay already collects plastic  waste from the beaches  it has benefits it has helped us we  collect the bottles and we put it in  other plastics that we collect from the  ocean we've made plastic blocks and  built a house from them  maimuna and her neighbours have joined  plastic recycling projects before  they showed us what they've built from  the waste they've collected  but the waste on the beaches is only  part of the problem  this device collects tiny plastic  particles from the sea  the samples will be sent to a lab to  find out if the water here is as  polluted as the coastline  bahati myoma has traveled from tanzania  to help with the research he says the  particles are disastrous for fish if a  plastic looks like  food for a certain speech it will be  consumed and we understand that plastic  cannot be digested this fish will end up  occurring either dying or their  reproductive reproductive ability will  be hampered  for more than a thousand years seasonal  winds carried traders in daos across the  indian ocean  today campaigners say the same winds are  bringing plastic waste from as far away  as china to these islands  they hope the flip floppy project will  help the people living on them to deal  with the growing challenge of plastic  waste  well malcolm webb joins us now live from  nairobi malcolm so what does it mean  that this resolution is now being passed  what will it do  the resolution uh commits the  signatories and there were no objections  all of the  governments represented there uh i've  agreed to this resolution and they  have committed to within the next two  years  negotiate and implement a legally  binding treaty  on plastics which is expected to  restrict plastic production  and to also address this issue of  plastic waste now the researchers in the  campaign as we just saw in the story  there  will take some weeks to process the data  to find out how much plastic pollution  is in that part of the indian ocean but  it's well known globally that plastic  pollution levels are soaring  increasing exponentially projected too  in the coming  decades plastics contaminated the food  chain it's destroying marine  environments  and campaigners are very excited they  say this resolution is a major step  forward in empowering uh governments to  tackle the companies that are profiting  uh from the status quo and legislate in  a way that will actually uh give a  chance uh of reducing this uh escalating  kind of pollution  all right malcolm webb for us in nairobi  thank you
 

